/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  bjerrum01_carbonate_pH
  ======================

  THE SPECIATION OF A CARBONATE SOLUTION ACROSS THE WHOLE pH RANGE -- the
  diagram every textbook draws, built the way Choupo has to build it.

  THE ONE THING THAT MAKES THIS DIFFERENT FROM THE TEXTBOOK VERSION: here
  the pH is NOT an axis you set -- and that is a CHOICE this case makes, in
  one word, which you can read three screens down.  A textbook draws the
  fractions against pH by substituting pH into the mass-action expressions,
  which is arithmetic and always works.  Choupo can do that too: `pH 7.8;`
  is a legal declaration, it fixes [H+], and it DROPS the electroneutrality
  row -- the run then reports the net charge the composition carries
  (`pH GIVEN -- this is the net charge the analysis carries`) instead of
  forcing it to zero.  That mode exists because a laboratory sheet reports
  a MEASURED pH, which is a datum.  It is not what this case wants.
  Every op below declares `pH solve;` instead, so [H+] joins the unknowns
  and the charge balance is the equation that closes the system.  The price
  of that choice is the whole lesson: each point had to be a beaker
  somebody could weigh out, the 44 of them differ only in what is IN the
  beaker, and the x-axis is a RESULT, exactly as it is in a laboratory.

  WHAT IS ACTUALLY IN EACH BEAKER.  Total carbonate is held at 1 mmol/kg
  throughout.  The 44 points differ only in how much strong acid or strong
  base sits beside it, and each one is a real neutral mixture somebody
  could weigh out:

      Cl / C_T = 5 ... 0.05   hydrochloric acid added to dissolved CO2
      Na / C_T = 0            dissolved CO2 alone          (H2CO3)
      Na / C_T = 1            sodium bicarbonate           (NaHCO3)
      Na / C_T = 2            sodium carbonate             (Na2CO3)
      Na / C_T = 2 ... 8      sodium carbonate + caustic soda

  That is what `totalsBasis stoichiometric;` declares below: these totals
  are neutral-molecule feeds, so the water-analysis charge-imbalance check
  -- which is right for a LAB SHEET, where an imbalance means somebody
  mismeasured -- does not apply.  Here the imbalance IS the titrant, and
  the electroneutrality equation IS the weak-acid pH.  Declaring it changes
  no number: it changes what the engine says about them.

  WHAT TO LOOK FOR, in the order a student should look:

  1.  THE TWO CROSSOVERS LAND ON THE TWO pK's, AND NOBODY PUT THEM THERE.
      CO2(aq) and HCO3- are equal at pH 6.341; the record's log K1 is
      6.352.  HCO3- and CO3-- are equal at pH 10.262; log K2 is 10.329.
      Both crossover pH's are INTERPOLATED between the two beakers that
      bracket them -- the axis is 44 sampled points, not a continuum, and
      no beaker lands exactly on a crossing.
      Neither pK was used as an input to any point -- they are the mass-
      action constants inside the network, and the crossover is where the
      titration happens to arrive.  The small shift DOWN in both is the
      activity coefficients: gamma_CO3 is 0.90 at the first crossover and
      0.81 at the second, while a textbook plot takes every gamma as 1.

  2.  THE THREE FRACTIONS DO NOT SUM TO ONE.  They dip to 0.99966 through
      the middle of the range, and the missing carbon is NOT round-off: it
      is `NaHCO3aq`, the sodium-bicarbonate ION PAIR, which the curated
      chemistry carries and the three-curve drawing has no place for.  The
      titrant is not a spectator.  Look at the `m_NaHCO3aq` diagnostic --
      and note where it is ABSENT: the two beakers with no sodium in them
      (the acid side, and Na/C_T = 0) have no such species to report, and
      the engine REFUSES to report a species that is not in the table
      rather than printing a zero.  Absent and zero are different claims.

  3.  THE ACTIVITY COEFFICIENT OF THE DIVALENT ION MAKES A U.  gamma_CO3
      runs 0.74 at the acid end, up to 0.98 near Na/C_T = 0 where the
      ionic strength is lowest, and back down to 0.67 at the alkaline end
      -- because BOTH ends of a titration are loaded with strong
      electrolyte and only the middle is dilute.  A textbook diagram takes
      every gamma as 1 at every pH, which is the one assumption that is
      worst exactly where the diagram is usually read.

  4.  THE SATURATION INDICES ARE THERE ALL ALONG.  Every point reports
      SI_nahcolite / SI_natron / SI_trona -- how far the solution is from
      precipitating a sodium carbonate mineral.  At 1 mmol/kg they stay far
      below zero, which is why this stays a clean acid-base diagram; make
      C_T a hundred times larger and the diagram acquires a ceiling.

  THE CONSTANTS, and their lineage: log K1 = 6.352, log K2 = 10.329 and
  Kw = 1e-14 at 25 C, from the USGS PHREEQC database (public domain), which
  is where data/standards/chemistry/{CO2aq,CO3}-formation.dat and
  water-dissociation.dat come from.  Each carries the van't Hoff dH and the
  full analytic log K(T), so this whole diagram can be redrawn at another
  temperature and both pK's will move -- change `temperature` below and
  watch which crossover moves further.

  NOT VALIDATED AGAINST MEASUREMENT.  This case is a STRUCTURAL witness: it
  shows that the engine's speciation network, its activity model and its
  charge balance compose into the diagram the chemistry predicts.  No
  titration data is compared against; the two crossovers landing near the
  two pK's is an internal consistency check on the assembly, not agreement
  with an experiment.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

//  THREE NUMBERS, ONE HOME EACH.  `$variables` are resolved at LOOKUP time,
//  so every one of the 44 operations below reads the SAME temperature and the
//  SAME total carbonate -- and a knob that turns one of them turns it once.
//  Written out 44 times over, this file would carry 44 homes for one fact,
//  which is the arity sin in a case file rather than in a data record.
variables
{
    //  Total carbonate, held across the whole titration.
    C_T   0.001 mol/kg;

    //  Temperature.  Every constant in this diagram carries a van't Hoff dH
    //  and a full analytic log K(T), so BOTH pK's move when this moves --
    //  turn it and watch which one moves further.
    T     298.15 K;

    //  A BACKGROUND-ELECTROLYTE KNOB BELONGS HERE AND IS NOT HERE YET.
    //  Raising the ionic strength at a fixed pK is the OTHER way this diagram
    //  moves, and separating it from temperature is the point of having two
    //  knobs.  Measured, so the next attempt starts from facts rather than
    //  from scratch: 0.5 mol/kg of KNO3 shifts the first crossover from
    //  pH 6.341 to 6.177 and drops gamma_CO3 from 0.903 to 0.289 -- the
    //  effect is large and worth teaching.  Potassium is the wrong carrier
    //  (the curated chemistry has KHCO3aq, which at 0.5 mol/kg holds 5.4 % of
    //  ALL the carbon, so the three fractions would sum to 0.946 and the
    //  diagram would quietly be about something else); LITHIUM has no such
    //  record, which is a statement about this reaction list and NOT about
    //  nature -- LiHCO3(aq) exists and is weak, and a diagram is complete
    //  only up to the reactions somebody wrote down.
    //
    //  THE TWO TOOL DEFECTS THAT BLOCKED IT (found 2026-08-28) WERE FIXED
    //  ON 2026-08-30, both proved on a scratch copy of THIS case:
    //  (1) bin/choupo-import now resolves `$variable` references before its
    //      regex harvest (`Li $bg;` seeds species/Li.dat exactly as the
    //      literal does), and
    //  (2) a sealed case whose reach GROWS re-seals by re-running
    //      bin/choupo-import: a REFUSED staged run now reads the consumption
    //      ledger first, learns the catalogue records the run asked for,
    //      and re-stages -- so the engine's "re-run bin/choupo-import"
    //      advice is finally true, and no manual manifest surgery exists to
    //      document.
    //  What remains is the CURATION half, which is the honest blocker: the
    //  chemistry list has no LiHCO3aq record, so a lithium background would
    //  hold its carbon fractions clean only up to the reactions somebody
    //  wrote down -- adding the knob is now a curation decision, not a tool
    //  fight.
}

operations
(
    //  ACID SIDE -- HCl added to dissolved CO2 (Na absent).
    { name acid_5p0; type speciate; analyticalTotals { Cl 0.005000 mol/kg; HCO3 $C_T; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; temperature $T; diagSpecies ( CO2aq HCO3 CO3 ); output { file acid_5p0.csv; } }
    { name acid_3p0; type speciate; analyticalTotals { Cl 0.003000 mol/kg; HCO3 $C_T; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; temperature $T; diagSpecies ( CO2aq HCO3 CO3 ); output { file acid_3p0.csv; } }
